---
name: check
description: Use when the user invokes $check after implementation or remediation, or asks for QA, review, screenshots, or test validation. Grades the accepted scope PASS, WARN, or BLOCK with proof. Also runs a strict maintainability (thermo-nuclear) code-quality bar and an AI-slop cleanup pass.
---

# check

SprintFlow's QA and validation boundary. Emit the QA plan first, run agent QA plus focused validation, collect proof, and decide whether the work can enter release gating.

## Use When

- `$build` finishes an implementation slice, `$fix` returns from remediation, or the user invokes `$check` for QA, review, screenshots, or test validation.

## What To Do

1. Identify the accepted scope, issue-board slice, and latest changed files.
2. Emit a QA plan before validation: scope under test, risk areas, commands, manual/browser/visual checks, review expectations, and PASS/WARN/BLOCK criteria.
3. Run the agent QA pass: behavior, regressions, missing tests, security-sensitive changes, user-facing evidence. Smallest meaningful validation first, broaden only when risk or blast radius requires. Apply the Code Quality Bar (Thermo-Nuclear) and run the Deslop Pass below over the accepted scope from step 1 (dirty patch, branch diff, or PR scope under review); both are review/flagging at `$check`, not remediation.
4. For write-heavy implementation from `$build`/`$fix`, verify handoff evidence before grading: completed Codex receipt, pending Codex receipt, or explicit fallback reason. Not required for planning, review-only, scouting, summarization, browser/system-dialog, credential-gated, doc-only, or trivial single-file inline edits.
5. Code-changing slice with no RED-then-GREEN evidence and no mechanical-edit or already-tested justification -> grade no higher than `WARN`, route to `$fix`.
6. Classify the diff before grading: code-changing means source, tests, build config, dependency manifests, scripts, CI, app/runtime config, or generated runtime/build artifacts. Treat unknown classes as code-changing until proven doc-only/non-code. For code-changing, branch, or PR targets, run Codex review per Codex Review Closeout below once reviewable and before final grading.
7. For frontend/UI changes, require appropriate browser or visual proof before `PASS`.
8. Classify findings against `~/.codex/skills/references/matt-first-routing.md` before choosing a direct result.
9. Grade applicable gates/nets from `~/.codex/skills/references/gates-and-nets-production.md`; trigger Trail of Bits helpers for security/API/config diffs, static scan needs, dependency risk, or agentic CI risk.
10. If OpenSpec is present or the work is OpenSpec-backed, apply `~/.codex/skills/references/openspec-sprintflow.md`: run `OPENSPEC_TELEMETRY=0 openspec validate --all --strict --json` and `OPENSPEC_TELEMETRY=0 openspec status --change <id> --json`; a failed `--strict`, or a `missingDeps`/blocked task marked complete, is a BLOCK. Verify artifact/code drift, and treat expanded `/opsx:verify` as optional advisory evidence only when installed.
11. For high-risk or unclear validation, Oracle may supplement using `/Users/testadmin/.codex/skills/references/oracle-advisory-escalation.md`; advisory only, never a substitute for tests, `$codex-review`, local verification, `$gate`, or release authority.
12. When an active goal uses `check-clean`, keep validation transcript-visible: QA plan, exact checks, Codex Review status, and grading evidence for the runtime goal evaluator.
13. Grade the result as `PASS`, `WARN`, or `BLOCK`. Code Quality Bar presumptive blockers and accepted slop feed `WARN`/`BLOCK` per the Approval Bar mapping below.
14. Post the `$check` progress comment per the Ticket Comment Cadence in `~/.codex/skills/references/sprintflow-core.md` (the `PASS`/`WARN`/`BLOCK` grade, checks run, Codex Review status), gated not fatal; on missing credentials or Plan Mode surface the exact comment text, set `ticket_comment_blocker`, and continue.

## Code Quality Bar (Thermo-Nuclear)

Review implementation quality, not just correctness, against the full standard in `/Users/testadmin/.codex/skills/check/references/thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review.md` (load and apply it). Be ambitious: hunt behavior-preserving "code-judo" moves that delete whole branches, helpers, modes, or layers and make the change feel inevitable in hindsight. Prefer deleting complexity over rearranging it. Be direct and demanding; do not soften major maintainability issues into nits.

Apply that reference for the standards, primary review questions, what to flag aggressively, and preferred remedies. Treat each presumptive blocker in its Approval Bar (file pushed past 1k lines, ad-hoc branching that tangles a flow, feature checks scattered across shared code, unnecessary abstraction/wrapper/cast churn, canonical-helper duplication or wrong-layer logic, and a visible-but-missed code-judo simplification) as presumptive `WARN`/`BLOCK` unless the author justifies it clearly; route blockers to `$fix`.

## Deslop Pass

Over the accepted scope under review (the dirty patch, branch diff, or PR scope from step 1, not a blind branch-vs-main diff), flag AI-generated slop. At `$check` this is review/flagging by default (check grades, it does not own remediation); actionable slop becomes a finding routed to `$fix`.

Focus areas:

- Extra comments that are unnecessary or inconsistent with local style.
- Defensive checks or try/catch blocks abnormal for trusted code paths.
- Casts to `any` used only to bypass type issues.
- Deeply nested code that should be simplified with early returns.
- Other patterns inconsistent with the file and surrounding codebase.

Guardrails: keep behavior unchanged unless fixing a clear bug; prefer minimal, focused edits over broad rewrites; keep the slop summary concise (1-3 sentences).

## Ponytail Over-Engineering Pass

Advisory, WARN-only: run `/ponytail-review` (user-typed) or the equivalent over-engineering review on the accepted diff. Surface complexity findings (delete / stdlib / native / yagni / shrink) as WARN-level notes only -- NEVER escalate them to BLOCK. Correctness, security, and multi-tenant `merchant_id` review stay separate and authoritative; ponytail-review is complexity-only.

## Codex Review Closeout

MUST invoke `$codex-review` when `$check` is grading any code-changing work, non-trivial code edits, a dirty patch, a branch, or a PR. Advisory review evidence, not release authority. Run once the smallest useful validation proves the checkout is reviewable, via Bash: `node "$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT/scripts/codex-companion.mjs" review` or `$codex-review`. `/codex:review` is user-typed only -- do NOT invoke via `Skill(codex:review)` or model-driven SlashCommand (`disable-model-invocation`).

- Code-changing work cannot receive `$check PASS` without Codex Review evidence or an explicit human override. Missing/unresolved/unavailable after the allowed fallback and retry -> grade `WARN` or `BLOCK` with the exact blocker.
- A code-changing `PASS` must include exactly one status token in Evidence: `Codex Review: clean|fallback-clean|fallback-findings|unresolved|skipped-with-reason`.
- If `$codex-review` is not exposed by name, do not skip review: load `/Users/testadmin/.codex/skills/codex-review/SKILL.md` and run `/Users/testadmin/.codex/skills/codex-review/scripts/codex-review --timeout-seconds 600` from the target checkout; if that path/helper is missing, report it and use the Subagent Review Fallback when permitted.
- Run one bounded helper invocation per `$check` (may run `codex review` plus the `codex exec` fallback). If it reports `codex-review unresolved` from a timeout or ambiguous verdict, make exactly one bounded helper retry with the same target and 600s budget. Verdicts: `clean`/`fallback-clean` -> clean review evidence (report any preceding `primary-timeout` in Evidence); `fallback-findings` -> actionable, grade `WARN`/`BLOCK`, route to `$fix`, re-run; unresolved after retry -> stop spinning, report the blocker, and choose one path only: explicit human override accepting missing Codex evidence, user-run interactive `/code-review high`, or Oracle-style advisory review per `/Users/testadmin/.codex/skills/references/oracle-advisory-escalation.md`. Do not run raw `codex review --uncommitted` or a third helper loop after timeout.
- No review target, verifier-confirmed doc-only/non-code, or clean `main` -> record `Codex Review: skipped-with-reason` with the reason (never use the doc-only skip for code-changing files). Report rejected/deferred findings briefly.
- Do not let Oracle or `$codex-review` bypass tests, visual proof, Matt-first routing, `$gate`, `$land`, or `$close`.

## Workflow Review Escalation

When invoking the review workflow, pass args `{ ticket, changedFiles, diffCmd, uiChange (true for frontend/UI scope), securityTouch, merchantIdTouch, releaseBound }`.

`$check` invocation is Workflow opt-in. Auto-invoke a review workflow when scope is non-trivial (>3 changed files, security/auth/data-touching, multi-tenant `merchant_id` paths, or release-bound): per-cluster scans on Haiku, security probe and PASS/WARN/BLOCK judge on Opus (effort high), independent review on the Codex lane (`codex-companion.mjs review`) with an Opus High fallback, and security/auth/data or multi-tenant `merchant_id` findings cleared by the Opus orchestrator inline (no in-workflow model clears release/security risk). Only verified findings enter grading; it supplements but never replaces the mandatory Codex Review, visual proof, or Matt-first routing. Trivial scope (<=2 files, mechanical) skips it for the single-subagent fallback. Report `Workflow Review: run|skipped + reason` in Evidence.

## Subagent Review Fallback

Use only after the required Codex Review helper is missing, fails to start, or the one bounded Codex Review retry still reports `codex-review unresolved`.

- Spawn one read-only native subagent review (`model: 'opus', effort: 'high'`; route any security/auth/data or multi-tenant `merchant_id` verdict to the Opus orchestrator inline) when subagents are available and permitted: review the dirty patch, branch diff, or PR scope, do not edit files, return actionable findings with severity and exact file/line refs. Continue local validation meanwhile; do not rerun Codex Review just because it is running.
- Accepted actionable issues -> grade `WARN`/`BLOCK`, route to `$fix`. No blocking issues + otherwise-clean validation -> the unresolved helper remains residual risk; grade no higher than `WARN` unless the user explicitly accepts the fallback as sufficient. Subagents unavailable/not permitted -> report `Subagent Review: unavailable/not permitted` and keep the unresolved/missing-helper result as `WARN`.
- Final evidence must name `Codex Review` status and `Subagent Review` status separately.

## Route To Matt Skills

MUST invoke the matching Matt helper when its trigger fits:

- `$diagnose`/`$debug` for broken harnesses, flaky baselines, unclear or repeated failures.
- `$improve-codebase-architecture` when validation exposes module-boundary, locality, or test-seam friction.
- `$tdd`/`$fix` when a missing regression test should lock the remediation.
- `$codex-review` for code-changing, branch, or PR targets before final grading.

## Cursor overflow (Codex unavailable)

When Claude Code and Codex are rate-limited in Cursor:

- Parent stays on **Auto**; gather `git diff` + changed file contents first.
- **MUST** `Task(subagent_type: thermo-nuclear-review-subagent)` — GPT-5.5 via user agent override at `~/.cursor/agents/thermo-nuclear-review-subagent.md`.
- Thermos replaces Codex review per `~/.cursor/rules/review-gates.mdc`; hooks auto-continue spine after review via `followup_message`.
- Record the thermo-nuclear review delegate/model internally; surface it only when parser-safe output or routing internals are explicitly requested.

## Stop Conditions

- Do not make final release/security decisions; `$gate` owns that boundary.
- Do not move tickets to `Done`; `$close` owns completion.
- If evidence is missing but actionable, grade `WARN` and route to `$fix`.
- Do not clear a goal merely because checks were attempted. `check-clean` requires `PASS` with required proof; actionable `WARN/BLOCK` -> `$fix`, unclear proof -> `$debug`.

## Output

Compact Markdown:

- `Summary`: ticket/scope, risk, goal profile when active, and `PASS`, `WARN`, or `BLOCK`.
- `QA Plan`: scope under test, risk areas, commands, manual/visual checks, grading criteria.
- `Evidence`: agent QA result, diff classification (`code-changing` or `doc-only/non-code`), commands/checks run, Codex delegation receipt or fallback reason when required, Codex Review command or skip reason, Code Quality Bar result, Deslop Pass result, important results.
- `Ticket Comment`: posted check progress note (grade plus Codex Review status), or the deferred comment text plus `ticket_comment_blocker`.
- `Findings`: actionable findings (each with `id`, `severity`, `file:line`, one-line `summary`, and `source` as `codex-review|thermo|deslop|workflow`), mandatory Matt trigger decision when present, and accepted WARN/residual items under `Residual Risks`.
- `Next`: use `~/.codex/skills/references/next-skill-router.md`; include only `Next`, `Why`, `Evidence`, `Alternatives`, and real blockers by default. Keep lane/model/agent/session fields internal unless parser-safe output or routing internals are explicitly requested. `PASS -> $gate`, actionable `WARN/BLOCK -> $fix`, unclear failures -> `$debug`.
